            <ArticleTitle>Protein Z levels and prognosis in patients with acute coronary syndromes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1098-102</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Protein Z, a vitamin K-dependent glycoprotein, serves as a cofactor for the inhibition of activated coagulation factor X. During recent years, a role for low levels of protein Z in prothrombotic disorders such as ischemic stroke and acute coronary syndromes (ACS) has been reported. The aims of this study were to test changes in protein Z and their association with outcome at 1-year follow-up in 193 (150 male, 43 female) patients with ACS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Protein Z plasma levels were significantly lower (p&lt;0.0001) after 1 year [1,600 (28-3,736) ng/mL] compared to the baseline [1,695 (294-4,068) ng/mL]. Regression analysis showed a significant association between baseline protein Z below the 5th percentile of our control group and subsequent adverse cardiac events at follow-up (odds ratio 3.3; 95% CI 1.04-10.7; p=0.04). Moreover, Cox regression analysis showed that low protein Z levels at admission were significant predictors of major adverse cardiac events (cardiac death, non-fatal recurrent myocardial infarction, and need for target lesion revascularization) after 1 year (hazard risk 2.5; 95% CI 1.02-6.5, p=0.04).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our results show that in patients with ACS: 1) protein Z decreases moving from the acute to the convalescent phase; and 2) low levels of baseline protein Z are significantly associated with adverse outcome at 1-year follow-up.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
